---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/pages/about.css';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="AI For Good is a student-run organisation in Hong Kong, started in June 2023. We write policy recommendations for the government and run free tutoring for children from underprivileged families."

  pageClass="page-about">
  <section class="page-top">
    <div class="container prose">
      <p class="kicker">About</p>
      <h1>Policy and teaching, for the same reason</h1>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 1. What AFG is -->
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container prose">
      <p class="lead">
        AI For Good is a student-run organisation in Hong Kong, started in June
        2023. We do two things. We write recommendations on AI and economic
        policy and send them to the Hong Kong government, about twenty a year.
        We also run free tutoring for primary school children from
        underprivileged families, and have taught 350 of them so far.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 2. Why both — the connective idea -->
  <section class="why">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Why we do both</h2>
      </div>
      <blockquote class="why-claim">
        AI policy written by people who have never met the people it affects
        isn't good policy. The children we teach are the people it affects.
      </blockquote>
      <div class="prose why-body">
        <p>
          It isn't a coincidence that we do both. Sit in a classroom in Ho Man
          Tin and watch a child use an AI tutor for the first time, and you
          learn things a consultation paper won't tell you: which children
          already have a tutor at home, which ones have a device, and which ones
          are about to fall further behind because of a technology everyone
          keeps calling an equaliser.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most of our submissions start there. Teaching shows us where the gaps
          are. Research tells us whether a gap is a policy problem. Then the
          submission asks the government to fix it. It works the other way too:
          what we learn writing about education and labour policy changes what
          we think is worth teaching a ten-year-old. We'd rather be the people
          who have been in the room than the people who have only read about
          it.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 3. Origin -->
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>How it started</h2>
        <p>
          AI For Good was founded in June 2023 by <strong>Justin Lo</strong>,{' '}
          <strong>Ka Hei Ng</strong> and <strong>Oscar Zheng</strong>.{' '}
          <strong>David Dong</strong> and <strong>Jackie Xue</strong> joined
          later. We started outside school, as an independent think tank rather
          than a school club. Since then we've grown to more than 35 members and
          started working with schools across Hong Kong, which is how the
          tutoring programme reaches the children it does.
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Justin's own account, in the first person and left in his words.
           The voice shift from "we" to "I" is deliberate and attributed. -->
      <div class="founder-note">
        <div class="fn-part">
          <p class="fn-label">Why the policy work</p>
          <p>
            AI arrived in Hong Kong faster than the city could decide who it was
            for. I watched it show up in my school, then in my friends'
            internships, then in the government's plans, and the pattern was
            always the same: the people who already had the most access got it
            first. Nobody was choosing that. It was just what happened when
            nobody was asked to choose. I had spent two years learning how
            public money gets allocated and who ends up bearing the cost of a
            decision, and it seemed absurd to sit on that while the decisions
            were being made a few kilometres away. So we started writing to the
            people making them.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="fn-part">
          <p class="fn-label">Why the teaching</p>
          <p>
            This started as a group of us at school teaching each other AI,
            mostly by playing with it. We built things that didn't work and a
            few that did, and it was obvious how much you could get out of these
            tools once someone showed you the first step. Then it became obvious
            who wasn't getting shown. The children with the most to gain from AI
            were the least likely to ever be handed it, and that gap wasn't
            going to close on its own. So we took what we'd been doing for fun
            in a classroom and brought it to schools where nobody else was
            offering it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="fn-attrib">— Justin Lo, founder</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="origin-figure">
        <img
          src="/programs/team-outside-school.jpg"
          alt="Five AI For Good members outside Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School."
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>Outside Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School, where we teach.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4. What we do — two blocks -->
  <section class="tint">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="mb">What we do</h2>
      <div class="two-blocks">
        <div class="block block-policy">
          <h3>Policy</h3>
          <p>
            Every year we send about twenty written recommendations to the Hong
            Kong government. We've responded to every Policy Address and Budget
            consultation since 2024, and we're now working on a submission to
            the First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social Development
            (2026–2030). Our topics come out of the work itself: tax, AI
            governance, education, jobs and health. Over several submissions we
            argued for an inclusive AI programme, and the 2026–27 Budget set
            aside HK$50 million for AI for All.
          </p>
          <a class="link-quiet" href="/policy">Read our policy work →</a>
        </div>
        <div class="block block-teaching">
          <h3>Teaching</h3>
          <p>
            We run free tutoring for primary school children from
            underprivileged families in seven schools across Hong Kong. Our 43
            trained tutors teach four modules: maths, English, economics and
            science. Each one uses AI as a free, patient study partner. We've
            taught 350 children so far, and since 2024, 86% of our students have
            moved up at least one class band.
          </p>
          <a class="link-quiet" href="/teaching">See our teaching →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 5. How we work -->
  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="mb">How we work</h2>
      <div class="two-blocks">
        <div class="method">
          <p class="method-label">Writing a proposal</p>
          <p>
            First we talk to our members, then we research, then we write. Every
            proposal sets out the case for, then the case against, then our
            recommendation, always in that order. If the case against turns out
            to be stronger, we don't send it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="method">
          <p class="method-label">Running a lesson</p>
          <p>
            Every tutor is trained before they teach. They follow a written
            curriculum built around the four modules, so a child gets the same
            quality of lesson whichever school they're at and whoever is
            teaching.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 6. Impact — the account first, then the two outcomes it rests on -->
  <section class="impact">
    <div class="container">
      <h2 class="mb">Our impact</h2>
      <p class="impact-body">
        AI For Good started in 2023 as a small group of students. Today there
        are more than 35 of us, and we all believe young people can change
        things by speaking up about them. Each year we send around 20 policy
        recommendations to the Hong Kong government. We've taken part in the
        Policy Address and Budget consultations since 2024, and we're now
        contributing to the First Five-Year Plan for Economic and Social
        Development (2026–2030). Alongside the policy work, we've taught 350
        children from underprivileged families at our partner schools and
        trained 43 student tutors. Since 2024, 86% of the students we teach have
        moved up at least one class band. For us, the policy and the teaching
        come from the same belief: a better world gets built by people who
        decide not to wait.
      </p>
      <div class="outcomes">
        <div class="outcome outcome-policy">
          <p class="outcome-figure">HK$50M</p>
          <p class="outcome-label">
            allocated to AI for All in the 2026–27 Budget, the kind of inclusive
            AI programme we'd been arguing for
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="outcome outcome-teaching">
          <p class="outcome-figure">86%</p>
          <p class="outcome-label">
            of our students have moved up at least one class band since 2024
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 7. Partners -->
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>Who we work with</h2>
      <p>
        We teach with Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School, St. Barnabas' Home
        &amp; Society, Kennedy Town School and 10% Organisation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 8. Where we're going -->
  <section class="section-tight ahead">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>What's next</h2>
      <ul class="ahead-list">
        <li>
          <span class="ahead-tag ahead-policy">Policy</span>
          We're working on our submission to the First Five-Year Plan for
          Economic and Social Development (2026–2030), about how AI and economic
          policy can open up opportunities rather than narrow them.
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="ahead-tag ahead-teaching">Teaching</span>
          We're going back to Ho Man Tin Ling To Catholic School next year with
          all four modules and a new group of tutors.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 9. Get involved -->
  <section class="section-tight">
    <div class="container prose">
      <h2>Get involved</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Schools and organisations:</strong> if you work with children
        from underprivileged families and could use tutors, we'll bring the
        lessons, the tutors and the equipment.{' '}
        <a href="/get-involved">Get in touch</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Students:</strong> we're recruiting at Victoria Shanghai
        Academy. If you're into policy, economics or AI, we care more about what
        you want to do than about titles.{' '}
        <a href="/get-involved">Tell us what you'd like to work on</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
